.\" Man page for mygrep
.TH MYGREP 3 "2026-09-24" "libmyutils 0.4.1" "libmyutils Manual"
.SH NAME
mygrep \- find lines in a file that contain a string
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <stdio.h>
.B #include \(dqmyfilefunctions.h\(dq
.PP
.BI "int mygrep(FILE *" fp ", const char *" search_str ", char ***" matches );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B mygrep()
function reads the file stream
.I fp
line by line and collects every line that contains the string
.IR search_str .
.PP
The matching lines are copied into newly allocated memory, and a newly
allocated array of pointers to them is stored in
.IR *matches .
The file is read from its current position until end of file.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, returns the number of matching lines (0 if none).
Returns \-1 if any argument is NULL or if memory allocation fails.
.SH NOTES
The caller is responsible for freeing each string in
.I *matches
and then the array itself with
.BR free (3).
Lines longer than 1023 characters are read in parts.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
char **matches = NULL;
int n = mygrep(fp, "systems", &matches);
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    printf("%s", matches[i]);
    free(matches[i]);
}
free(matches);
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wordCount (3),
.BR strstr (3)
